# Python virtual environment -- recreate with:
#   python3 -m venv venv && source venv/bin/activate && pip install -r requirements.txt
venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Per-user runtime state -- your screen region, popup marker, and GUI
# settings are specific to your screen/game/preferences, not something to
# share or overwrite someone else's copy of. Each computer generates its
# own via the app itself (--select, --select-popup-marker, or just running
# the GUI once).
region.json
popup_marker.json
gui_settings.json

# Downloaded voice models -- these are large (Piper voices are 60-140MB
# each; Kokoro's model + voices file together are ~350MB) and GitHub
# rejects anything over 100MB outright, so none of this belongs in git
# regardless of size. See README.md's "Better voices" section for where to
# download them from. The ** matches any depth, so this covers both
# voices/model.onnx and voices/kokoro/model.onnx-style subfolders alike.
voices/**/*.onnx
voices/**/*.onnx.json
voices/**/*.bin

# PyInstaller build output (build_windows.py) -- the whole point of that
# script is that everyone builds their own .exe from source rather than
# one being committed or shipped in the repo. build/ is PyInstaller's
# scratch work area; dist/ is where the finished app actually ends up.
# The .spec file is auto-generated fresh by build_windows.py every run,
# so it doesn't need to be tracked either.
build/
dist/
game-text-speaker.spec
